Superficial abrasion from sliding on abrasive surface to forearm
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Punctate capillary oozing, Irregular shallow friction wound, Abrasive defensive slide mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-030
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful, and dirt-contaminated friction wound requiring urgent wound irrigation and barrier protection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a dirty friction abrasion on the upper limb following a slide on masonry. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to aggressively scrub and irrigate the wound, removing all embedded abrasive grit to prevent permanent traumatic tattooing and secondary infection before daytime clinical services became available.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I scraped my arm hard against the brick wall when I tripped; it’s stinging badly and covered in dirt.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a defensive slide mechanism, dragging the arm across a rough, unyielding masonry surface 45 minutes ago. The patient describes a shallow, raw friction wound with stinging and burning pain precisely localized to the denuded dermal area. There are small punctate areas of capillary oozing that coagulate rapidly upon exposure to air. The wound margins are irregular and non-linear, corresponding exactly to the area of contact friction. The patient notes the presence of minor exogenous surface contamination (superficial dust and grit).
Functional Status: Fully independent; actively protecting the arm from accidental brushing.
Social/Safety Concerns: None.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/76 | HR: 74 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, holding the arm away from the body to avoid clothing contact.
Physical Exam: Forearm examination reveals an irregular shallow friction wound, removing only the outermost stratum corneum and superficial epidermis. Punctate capillary oozing is present. Minor superficial grit is visualized on the wound surface. There is a complete absence of underlying structural damage; active and passive wrist and elbow mobility is fully preserved, strong, and painless. Normal neurovascular status is evaluated and confirmed distal to the abrasion site.
Environmental Observations: No significant hazards; patient tripped over a known, fixed step.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial abrasion to forearm - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep dermal laceration - Ruled out due to: Shallow, broad nature of the wound without a linear break penetrating into the subcutaneous fat layer.

Retained deep foreign body - Ruled out due to: Visual confirmation of exclusively superficial dust without deep puncture tracts.

Underlying radius/ulna fracture - Ruled out due to: Pain-free, strong active range of motion of the wrist and elbow, and complete absence of bony point tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Abrasions from masonry or asphalt require immediate differentiation between superficial skin loss and deep, contaminated lacerations. Bedside clinical assessment confirmed the superficial nature of the wound and completely ruled out underlying bony injury or deep tendon involvement. Urgent after-hours visitation was mandatory to perform highly effective mechanical wound scrubbing while the injury was acute, effectively removing all abrasive grit to prevent irreversible traumatic tattooing of the skin, a procedure that becomes significantly more difficult if delayed until daytime hours.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed rigorous wound cleansing using sterile saline, mild chlorhexidine soap, and a sterile gauze scrub to extract all visible masonry dust. Applied a thin layer of topical antiseptic ointment and a non-adherent sterile dressing. Tetanus booster administered.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wash the wound gently with mild soap and water once daily, patting it dry before applying a fresh, breathable dressing. This mechanical cleansing physically washes away surface bacteria and dead cells, actively preventing a scab infection and promoting faster healing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Non-adherent dressings (Melolin) and retention tape.
Follow-up: Routine GP review only necessary if the wound shows signs of infection or delayed healing after 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the local GP or Emergency Department if the forearm becomes significantly swollen, if a spreading red rash appears around the wound, or if it begins discharging thick yellow pus.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Abrasions often look worse on day two as a natural inflammatory exudate forms; this is a normal part of the healing process.
Clinical Course: A thin, dry scab will form over the next 48 hours. The wound will heal completely from the edges inward over 7 to 10 days, leaving a temporary pink discoloration.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention if the stinging pain suddenly changes to a deep, severe throbbing ache, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Thoroughly scrubbing the wound prevents dirt particles from healing permanently under the skin (traumatic tattooing) and removes the bacteria that cause infections.
Actionable Steps: Change the dressing daily. Do not pick at the scab once it forms, as this restarts the healing process and causes scarring. Keep the arm protected from direct sunlight while it heals.

